@prefix tgs:   <http://example.org/tgs#> .
@prefix thinkr: <http://example.org/thinkr#> .
@prefix owl:   <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#> .
@prefix rdfs:  <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#> .
@prefix skos:  <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#> .
@prefix dct:   <http://purl.org/dc/terms/> .
@prefix dcmitype: <http://purl.org/dc/dcmitype/> .
@prefix xsd:   <http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#> .

#############################################
# episodes-rr.ttl -- PILOT extraction, 2026-09-12.
# All 27 Reality Roundtable entities (tgs:PanelDiscussion), split out of
# episodes.ttl as a proof-of-concept for the real TGS/RR/Frankly
# three-way split. RR chosen as the pilot subject because it's the one
# real, complete, closed set (27 of 27 built, no gaps) -- no stub
# minting needed here, unlike TGS/Frankly will require.
#
# Sorted by episode number (not build order, not alphabetically) --
# the new standing convention going forward per direct instruction,
# 2026-09-12. No Series individuals exist for RR specifically as of
# this pilot, so no top-of-file series block is needed here; TGS/
# Frankly extraction will need one (at minimum tgs:Series.OilTrilogy
# and tgs:Series.AGuideToStayingHuman both touch Frankly).
#
# episodes.ttl itself is left completely untouched by this pilot --
# per direct instruction, nothing is deleted from the original until
# this extraction has been reviewed.
#############################################

<http://example.org/tgs#> a owl:Ontology ;
    owl:imports <http://example.org/thinkr#> ;
    rdfs:comment "Imports tgs-core for Protege compatibility — editing this file alone will resolve thinkr: classes/properties via the catalog file."@en .

tgs:PanelDiscussion.RR_1_ElectricVehicles a thinkr:Episode, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:createdDate "2026-08-13"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:updatedDate "2026-08-13"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.PanelDiscussion ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeSegment tgs:EpisodeSegment.RealityRoundtable ;
    skos:prefLabel "\"Electric Vehicles\": Arthur Berman, Simon Michaux & Pedro Prieto"@en ;
    skos:definition "The inaugural Reality Roundtable — Nate brings together 3 returning guests (petroleum geologist Art Berman, geometallurgist Simon Michaux, and solar/EROI analyst Pedro Prieto) to biophysically stress-test whether dramatically scaling electric vehicles is materially, economically, and infrastructurally achievable, or whether current EV enthusiasm is itself a form of energy blindness — covering mineral/mining constraints, grid capacity, real EROI figures, and whether EVs address the actual root problem or just substitute one narrow-boundary technology story for another."@en ;
    dct:created "2023-04-23"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2023-06-11"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.EconomicsAndMonetary, tgs:Subject.SystemsScience, tgs:Subject.Geopolitics ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    thinkr:hasGuest tgs:Persona.ArtBerman, tgs:Persona.SimonMichaux, tgs:Persona.PedroPrieto ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/episode/rr01-berman-michaux-prieto> ;
    thinkr:hasTranscript <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2023/06/RR01BermanMichauxPrietoTranscript.docx1_.pdf> ;
    thinkr:discusses tgs:Concept.EnergyReturnOnInvestment, tgs:Concept.EnergyBlindness ;
    dct:references tgs:Interview.TGS_3_OilBestAndWorstOfFuels, tgs:Interview.TGS_44_DieselAndTheBarrelOfOil, tgs:Interview.TGS_54_TheStealthAdjustmentOfOil, tgs:Interview.TGS_7_JoshFarleyAppearance1, tgs:Interview.TGS_29_JoshFarleyAppearance2 ;
    thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary "Largely a consensus-building conversation rather than a debate — the three guests, longtime friends and repeat TGS guests with overlapping biophysical/systems worldviews, converge on a shared thesis rather than argue against each other. Michaux opens with a detailed materials analysis showing that scaling EVs and renewables to meet stated EU 2030 targets would require multiples of known global reserves and annual production of key metals, even before accounting for grid capacity, hydrogen production, or the fossil fuels still required to make steel, plastic, and concrete. Berman and Prieto largely corroborate and extend this from their own domains rather than push back — Berman explicitly says 'I agree with you, I provided you data to support your perspective,' and adds that oil companies' underinvestment is a financial-market choice, not a resource-scarcity problem; Prieto brings real Spanish/German grid-stress data and EV-affordability numbers. The closest thing to friction is clarifying, not adversarial — Berman and Prieto probe Michaux on his 28-day storage-buffer assumption and the electric/hydrogen vehicle split, and he candidly revises and defends specific figures under that questioning, at one point calling the underlying 'is this sensible?' question 'the best argument for degrowth.' The one moment of genuine, if mild, divergence comes in the closing 'how could you be wrong' round: Michaux allows an unforeseen technological breakthrough could upend his numbers; Prieto reframes his own uncertainty as being about timing rather than direction ('sooner than later the system will collapse'); and Berman draws a sharper line between his own specific technical claims (which he says could be wrong) and the biodiversity/ecosystem decline data underlying the whole discussion (which he considers not meaningfully disputable). All three land on the same practical conclusion Nate frames at the top: current EV scaling plans reflect 'energy blindness' — a narrow, supply-side technology story substituting for the harder question of reducing consumption."@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Olivia Lazard (environmental peacemaker/mediator) was originally scheduled but couldn't attend due to travel issues, per Nate's own real substack write-up — deliberately NOT modeled as thinkr:hasGuest despite appearing in the show notes' own cross-reference list, since she never actually appeared. Her own real prior TGS episode is a separate, deferred candidate. Also deferred: Simon Michaux's own 2 solo TGS episodes (#19, #49, real URLs confirmed); Charles A. S. Hall (Pedro Prieto's real co-author on 'Spain's Photovoltaic Revolution,' already indirectly relevant to this graph via Concept.MaximumPowerPrinciple) and that book itself as a real Work — both real, neither built in this pass."@en ;
    .

tgs:PanelDiscussion.RR_2_DeeperEcology a thinkr:Episode, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:createdDate "2026-08-13"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:updatedDate "2026-08-13"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.PanelDiscussion ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeSegment tgs:EpisodeSegment.RealityRoundtable ;
    skos:prefLabel "\"Deep(er) Ecology\": William Rees, Nora Bateson & Rex Weyler"@en ;
    skos:definition "Nate is joined by three friends — ecological economist William Rees (originator of Ecological Footprint Analysis), Nora Bateson, and Rex Weyler — for a first-principles examination of what ecology actually means beneath its textbook definition (emergence, self-organization, dissipative structures, evolution), why humanity's own outsized ecological footprint makes contraction biophysically inevitable rather than optional, and what an ecologically literate civilization might look like on the other side of it."@en ;
    dct:created "2023-06-27"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2023-07-09"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.EconomicsAndMonetary, tgs:Subject.SystemsScience, tgs:Subject.Geopolitics ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    thinkr:hasGuest tgs:Persona.WilliamRees, tgs:Persona.NoraBateson, tgs:Persona.RexWeyler ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/episode/rr02-bateson-rees-weyler> ;
    thinkr:hasTranscript <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2023/07/BatesonReesWeyler.pdf> ;
    thinkr:discusses tgs:Concept.Overshoot, tgs:Concept.EcologicalFootprint, tgs:Concept.LimitsToGrowth ;
    dct:references tgs:Interview.TGS_10_NoraBateson ;
    skos:scopeNote "Real, further citable material found but NOT built in this pass: William Rees's own real prior TGS solo episode (confirmed via a YouTube link in this episode's own real show notes, https://youtu.be/LQTuDttP2Yg, no canonical TGS-site URL given). The direct citation at 56:52 ('contraction of the human species is inevitable') links straight to Club of Rome's own real Limits to Growth publication page — the exact same URL already used as Concept.LimitsToGrowth's own dcterms:source, independently corroborating that source's quality."@en ;
    thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary "The most substantively adversarial of the built RRs so far, though still warmly collegial — Nate's own opening explicitly names the contrast: 'people agree on deep ecology and the ecological predicament of human overshoot, yet take wildly different perspectives in how they view it.' Rees supplies the systems-scientific backbone (dissipative structures, thermodynamics, the mathematics of overshoot-and-crash), Weyler supplies historical/movement context and a harder moral edge (open frustration that environmental groups avoid words like 'overshoot'), and Bateson consistently redirects both toward relational process over prescription ('who can you be when you're with me' versus any engineered solution). The clearest real disagreement in any built RR happens here: when Rees says he'd prefer 1-2 billion people living well to 10 billion living in poverty, Nate directly pushes back ('I actually disagree... I think that's the default that we're headed towards'), and Rees concedes the practical point while holding his normative preference ('I agree it's easier, but I said IF you want to plan for something'). Bateson explicitly declines to fully endorse the contraction-is-inevitable framing Rees and Weyler share, prompting Nate to ask her directly whether she agrees — a rare moment where a built RR's host names a real, unresolved difference among guests rather than treating the conversation as settled consensus. Population and eugenics form the other real point of productive friction: Weyler raises population decline directly and names the taboo around it, Bateson immediately reframes the same territory through care and relational support rather than numbers, explicitly naming the eugenics risk in framing it any other way — not a rebuttal of Weyler's point, but a genuine redirection of its terms that he doesn't contest."@en ;
    .

tgs:PanelDiscussion.RR_3_UnlearningEconomics a thinkr:Episode, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:createdDate "2026-08-13"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:updatedDate "2026-08-13"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.PanelDiscussion ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeSegment tgs:EpisodeSegment.RealityRoundtable ;
    skos:prefLabel "\"Unlearning Economics\": Jon Erickson, Josh Farley, Steve Keen, and Kate Raworth"@en ;
    skos:definition "Nate brings together four heterodox economists — two of them his own real PhD advisors, Jon Erickson and Josh Farley — each given roughly 6 minutes to name one core assumption of mainstream Econ 101 they consider fundamentally broken: Erickson on Homo economicus and the rational-actor model, Keen on energy's near-total absence from macroeconomic theory, Farley on the assumption that markets efficiently allocate essential resources, and Raworth on GDP-centric growth as the discipline's implicit endpoint. The stated goal is blunt: roughly 235 million university students worldwide are being taught a model of the economy that doesn't track physical reality, and the episode is explicitly framed as something to show in classrooms."@en ;
    dct:created "2023-07-18"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2023-08-13"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.EconomicsAndMonetary, tgs:Subject.SystemsScience, tgs:Subject.Geopolitics ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    thinkr:hasGuest tgs:Persona.JonErickson, tgs:Persona.JoshFarley, tgs:Persona.SteveKeen, tgs:Persona.KateRaworth ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/episode/rr03-erickson-farley-raworth-keen> ;
    thinkr:hasTranscript <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2023/08/RR03EricksonFarleyRaworthKeenTranscript.docx.pdf> ;
    thinkr:discusses tgs:SchoolOfThought.DoughnutEconomics, tgs:SchoolOfThought.PlanetaryBoundaries, tgs:Concept.EnergyReturnOnInvestment ;
    skos:scopeNote "Real, further citable material found but NOT built in this pass: all 4 guests' own real prior TGS solo episodes are now confirmed with real URLs (Erickson #45, Keen #30, both via RR-3's own real show notes; Raworth's via YouTube, no canonical TGS-site URL). Real, specific named academic references that could become their own citation-only Personas if this thread is revisited: Elinor Ostrom (Nobel laureate, commons governance), Thorstein Veblen, E.O. Wilson (Consilience) — none built here."@en ;
    thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary "The most substantively adversarial-yet-collegial of the built RRs — the only one where two different guests explicitly announce they're playing 'devil's advocate' against a co-panelist's argument, not just adding texture. Raworth does it twice: first pressing Erickson on what actually replaces the rational-actor model once you've torn it down ('then what?'), and later challenging Farley's rationing proposal directly, arguing markets plus redistribution and a universal basic income would work better than rationing given rationing's own real historical failure modes (black markets, Soviet-style central planning). Farley doesn't concede — he counters with a specific alternative (guaranteed access to essential goods specifically, not a general UBI, because essential-good prices are too volatile for a fixed income to track), and the two continue trading specifics rather than one yielding. Keen plays devil's advocate 'in a different direction,' agreeing entirely with Erickson's critique of neoclassical economics but pushing further — arguing the theory fails on its own internal mathematical terms even before you get to its empirical failures. The clearest real disagreement, though, isn't among the guests but in how each answers Nate's own closing question — can any of this actually be taught at scale? Keen is bluntly pessimistic ('I don't think they can'), Farley says he 'sadly agrees a little bit' but still points to concrete, working programs, and Raworth explicitly frames herself as 'leaning into possibility,' citing a real curriculum win (the Doughnut now appearing in an International Baccalaureate economics textbook) as evidence change is already happening, however slowly. All four converge without disagreement on Raworth's own closing 'Embedded Economy' diagram — Erickson calls it 'not rocket science' as a genuine compliment, and Raworth explicitly welcomes that as the whole point."@en ;
    .

tgs:PanelDiscussion.RR_4_TitanicOceans a thinkr:Episode, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:createdDate "2026-08-13"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:updatedDate "2026-08-13"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.PanelDiscussion ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeSegment tgs:EpisodeSegment.RealityRoundtable ;
    skos:prefLabel "\"Titanic Oceans\": Daniel Pauly, Antonio Turiel, Peter Ward"@en ;
    skos:definition "Nate brings together three real friends of his — fisheries scientist Daniel Pauly, physical oceanographer Antonio Turiel, and paleobiologist Peter Ward — who have never met each other before this recording, to examine the ocean's overlooked role in the unfolding predicament: industrial overfishing sustained by subsidies, the physical mechanics and real risk of AMOC slowdown or collapse, and deep-time paleontological analogs (Canfield oceans, the Permian and PETM mass extinctions) for what a fully anoxic, stratified ocean would mean. Closes on a direct, emotionally heavy question submitted in writing by DJ White (unable to attend as originally planned) about cetacean extinction."@en ;
    dct:created "2023-07-31"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2023-09-10"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.EconomicsAndMonetary, tgs:Subject.SystemsScience, tgs:Subject.Geopolitics ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    thinkr:hasGuest tgs:Persona.DanielPauly, tgs:Persona.AntonioTuriel, tgs:Persona.PeterWard ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/episode/rr04-pauly-turiel-ward> ;
    thinkr:hasTranscript <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2023/09/RR04PaulyTurielWard.docx.pdf> ;
    thinkr:discusses tgs:EarthSystemComponent.AMOC ;
    skos:scopeNote "DJ White was originally invited as a fourth panelist but could not attend; Nate reads his own written question aloud instead — DJ White is already a real, built Persona in this graph (from Frankly_20/RR-24 work). This transcript independently reconfirms, a second time this session, that DJ White 'co-wrote three books' with Nate — the same real, still-unresolved detail first flagged during the RR-10 build (this graph currently only has 2 of the 3: Work.RealityBlind and 'The Bottlenecks of the 21st Century,' the latter itself not yet built as a Work individual). This episode's own real show notes independently confirm DJ White's own real prior TGS episode: #51. Real, further citable material found but NOT built in this pass: all 3 guests' own real prior TGS solo episodes (Pauly #15, Turiel #65, Ward #8)."@en ;
    thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary "Uniformly convergent and unusually warm — three genuine friends of Nate's, each bringing a different real disciplinary lens (Pauly's fisheries/subsidy economics, Turiel's physical oceanography, Ward's deep-time paleobiology) that consistently reinforce rather than compete with each other. The clearest human moment comes early and unscripted: Pauly, midway through the conversation, suddenly realizes he's been citing Ward's own book for years without ever having met him ('I said that I don't know Peter Ward, but I was wrong. I use your book all the time... I just didn't connect at all') — a small-world moment neither anticipated. Ward's own 'Medea Hypothesis' (life as structurally self-destructive, an explicit rebuttal to James Lovelock's Gaia Hypothesis) becomes the interpretive frame the whole conversation converges toward by its second half, culminating in Ward's own direct line: 'This is Medea. This is not Gaia... This is life doing it to itself.' The episode's emotional center is its ending: Nate reads aloud, in full, a written question DJ White had prepared before being unable to attend as originally planned — about the likely extinction of most large-brained cetacean species within this century — and all three guests respond with real, unrehearsed weight rather than analytical distance, Pauly describing starving, rib-visible dolphins in the Mediterranean he 'never thought I would see.' Nate's own closing words name the tone directly: 'human vulnerability and honesty,' and his reluctance to end the call is itself part of the episode's real content, not just a sign-off."@en ;
    .

tgs:PanelDiscussion.RR_5_FoodAndCommunityInTheRuins a thinkr:Episode, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:createdDate "2026-08-13"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:updatedDate "2026-08-13"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.PanelDiscussion ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeSegment tgs:EpisodeSegment.RealityRoundtable ;
    skos:prefLabel "Food & Community in the Ruins: Dougald Hine, Chris Smaje, Pella Thiel"@en ;
    skos:definition "Nate brings together three first-time guests, already personal friends by the time of recording — social thinker Dougald Hine (Dark Mountain Project, 'At Work in the Ruins'), small-farm writer Chris Smaje ('A Small Farm Future'), and Swedish ecologist/activist Pella Thiel (Transition Network) — to explore what rebuilding local food systems and genuine community actually looks like on the way toward a lower-energy, more localized future, organized around Hine's own framing of finding worthwhile 'work in the ruins' of a world that is ending, without mistaking that ending for the end of the world entirely."@en ;
    dct:created "2023-10-03"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2023-10-15"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.EconomicsAndMonetary, tgs:Subject.SystemsScience, tgs:Subject.Geopolitics ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    thinkr:hasGuest tgs:Persona.DougaldHine, tgs:Persona.ChrisSmaje, tgs:Persona.PellaThiel ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/episode/rr05-hine-smaje-thiel> ;
    thinkr:hasTranscript <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2023/10/RR05Transcript.docx.pdf> ;
    dct:references tgs:Persona.VanessaAndreotti ;
    skos:scopeNote "This episode's own real reference to 'Vanessa Machado de Oliveira' and her book 'Hospicing Modernity' is the same real person already built in this graph as tgs:Persona.VanessaAndreotti (her full/maiden name) — not a new individual. Real, further citable material found but NOT built in this pass: Pella Thiel's own real later solo TGS episode (#121); Federico Campagna, Paul Richards, Ivan Illich, and Ed Gillespie are all real, directly cited thinkers/collaborators not bootstrapped here."@en ;
    thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary "Uniformly warm and complementary, distinct in one real way from most other built RRs: the three guests were already personal friends before this recording (Nate had stayed at Pella Thiel's farm helping with lambing the night before, and had met both her and Hine in person in Sweden weeks earlier), so the conversation reads less like three experts meeting for the first time and more like a continuation of an existing relationship. The three framings are genuinely complementary rather than competing: Hine supplies the mythic/narrative register (his own 'at work in the ruins' framing, by way of the Italian philosopher Federico Campagna), Smaje supplies grounded agrarian-political realism (skeptical of both techno-fix optimism and any guru promising shortcuts, insistent on the specific, unglamorous politics of land access), and Thiel supplies lived, embodied community organizing (the real 'Potato Appeal' campaign she ran during COVID, modeled directly on a real 1917 Swedish uprising). The one small moment of real, friendly cross-checking rather than disagreement: Thiel attributes an idea to 'Paul Kingsnorth,' and Hine gently corrects her in real time ('It's one of his neighbors. It's Mark Boyle') — the kind of small factual collaboration that happens between people who already know each other well, not a substantive disagreement. Nate's own asides are genuinely personal throughout (his own visceral reaction to the guests' articulateness, his own coined phrase 'pro-social prepping'), and the episode closes on an unusually tender note — Hine speaking directly about his own son and the conversations he has with people weighing whether to become parents 'in a time like this.'"@en ;
    .

tgs:PanelDiscussion.RR_6_FossilFreeFoodSystems a thinkr:Episode, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:createdDate "2026-08-13"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:updatedDate "2026-08-13"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.PanelDiscussion ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeSegment tgs:EpisodeSegment.RealityRoundtable ;
    skos:prefLabel "Fossil Free Food Systems: Jason Bradford, Andrew Millison, Vandana Shiva, Daniel Zetah"@en ;
    skos:definition "Nate reunites four returning guests — farmer/biologist Jason Bradford (Post Carbon Institute), permaculture educator Andrew Millison (Oregon State University), physicist-turned-agroecology-activist Vandana Shiva (Navdanya), and Minnesota regenerative farmer Daniel Zetah — around one central question: could 8+ billion people be fed with minimal fossil fuel inputs, and what would that actually require in labor, land, culture, and policy? The conversation moves from de-urbanization and village-scale redesign through Shiva's real longitudinal soil-nutrition data to closing visions of each guest's own bioregion in 2050."@en ;
    dct:created "2023-10-10"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2023-11-12"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.EconomicsAndMonetary, tgs:Subject.SystemsScience, tgs:Subject.Geopolitics ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    thinkr:hasGuest tgs:Persona.JasonBradford, tgs:Persona.AndrewMillison, tgs:Persona.VandanaShiva, tgs:Persona.DanielZetah ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/episode/rr06-bradford-millison-shiva-zetah> ;
    thinkr:hasTranscript <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2023/11/RR06BradfordMillisonShivaZetahTranscript.docx.pdf> ;
    skos:scopeNote "William Rees is directly cross-referenced by Bradford ('a friend of yours, Nate, and mine') via the exact same real prior-episode YouTube URL already used for tgs:Persona.WilliamRees during this session's RR-2 build (https://youtu.be/LQTuDttP2Yg) — independent confirmation, not a new fact. Real, further citable material found but NOT built in this pass: real later solo TGS appearances for both Bradford (2026, Farming Clubs) and Millison (TGS #178, 2025); Bradford's real prior episode #76 is shared with Zetah's own prior appearance (a joint episode, not two separate ones — worth flagging as a real, unusual case of two guests sharing one numbered prior episode, though not verified further in this pass)."@en ;
    thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary "Highly convergent — Jason Bradford's own mid-episode summary names it directly: 'I think we're all kind of singing the same song here.' The one moment of genuine, explicit reframing rather than agreement comes from Daniel Zetah, who opens his turn by directly challenging Nate's own question: 'I would actually like to just challenge the question itself' — arguing the premise that the current fossil-fueled system is even 'feeding the world' well is itself false, given documented malnutrition and obesity coexisting under it. A second, milder real disagreement is one of degree rather than direction: Bradford's opening statement calls for depopulating cities outright, while Millison pushes back gently that 'not all cities are created equal,' pointing to cities historically sited on rivers and trade routes as potentially viable in a lower-energy future rather than uniformly abandoned. Shiva grounds the whole conversation in real, specific longitudinal data from her own farm (20 years of soil nitrogen, zinc, and manganese measurements comparing organic and chemical-input plots), giving the discussion an empirical anchor the other three build on rather than dispute. The episode's most self-aware moment is Bradford's own 'Shire' analogy via Lord of the Rings, which he flags himself as 'corny' before continuing anyway — Nate affectionately teases him for it at the close ('I knew you were corny') while praising his 'agro-ecological wisdom.' All four converge fully, without qualification, on ending agricultural subsidies and patents on seed/life as the necessary first policy steps — the clearest full consensus point in the episode."@en ;
    .

tgs:PanelDiscussion.RR_7_PovertyBlind a thinkr:Episode, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:createdDate "2026-08-13"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:updatedDate "2026-08-13"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.PanelDiscussion ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeSegment tgs:EpisodeSegment.RealityRoundtable ;
    skos:prefLabel "Stephanie Hoopes, Peter Kilde, Marc Perry, Dalitso Sulamoyo: \"Poverty Blind\""@en ;
    skos:definition "Nate convenes four anti-poverty practitioners — Stephanie Hoopes (creator of the ALICE financial-hardship measure), Peter Kilde (West CAP, Wisconsin, and Nate's real-life neighbor with a working relationship dating to 2010), Marc Perry (Community Action Inc. of Rock and Walworth Counties), and Dalitso Sulamoyo (Champaign County Regional Planning Commission, Illinois) — to examine US poverty beyond the federal poverty line, using Hoopes's ALICE metric to show 41% of US households cannot afford a bare-minimum survival budget despite being employed, often in multiple jobs. The conversation moves through disaster-response inequality (Katrina, Maria), racial disparity, and closes on what community-level resilience can do as resource constraints from the Great Simplification deepen."@en ;
    dct:created "2023-11-27"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2023-12-10"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.EconomicsAndMonetary, tgs:Subject.SystemsScience, tgs:Subject.Geopolitics ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    thinkr:hasGuest tgs:Persona.StephanieHoopes, tgs:Persona.PeterKilde, tgs:Persona.MarcPerry, tgs:Persona.DalitsoSulamoyo ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/episode/rr07-hoopes-kilde-perry-sulamoyo> ;
    thinkr:hasTranscript <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2023/12/RR07HoopesKildePerryandSulamoyoTranscript.docx.pdf> ;
    skos:scopeNote "Real, further citable material found but NOT built in this pass: Paul Ehrlich (real TGS #9, not yet built as a Persona) and Dennis Meadows (real TGS #12 — Meadows already exists as a real, built Persona in this graph, cited here only in passing, not as a guest, so his own record was not touched in this pass)."@en ;
    thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary "Highly collaborative among four practitioners who mostly extend rather than contest each other's points, anchored throughout by Hoopes's own ALICE data (41% of US households below a survival budget despite employment). The clearest moment of real, pointed directness rather than diplomatic framing comes from Marc Perry, describing the frustration in Black communities at having raised the same structural concerns for decades to what he calls 'deaf ears' — 'it took somebody white to say it in order for you to actually pay attention to it.' A second genuinely candid moment, flagged by the speaker himself as sensitive, is Dalitso Sulamoyo raising real community tension over resource allocation to recent asylum seekers versus longtime residents — he calls it 'quite a touchy issue' and does not soften it further. Peter Kilde grounds the episode's abstract inequality argument in specific documented history — a detailed comparison of Hurricane Katrina and Hurricane Maria's radically unequal disaster responses, including real death tolls and recovery-fund disparities — building on his own extensive, real personal connection to New Orleans and a professional history with peak-oil and resource-depletion work (ASPO-USA board membership) that predates and directly parallels Nate's own subject matter. Marc Perry's own personal disclosure — growing up materially poor but 'rich' in family and community, not realizing he was poor until told — offers a lived counterpoint to the episode's data-heavy first half. The two guests' closing interpretive frames diverge memorably without conflicting: Kilde insists poverty is fundamentally 'a math problem,' not a behavioral one, while Perry frames the culture's complacency through Huxley's Brave New World rather than Orwell's 1984 — people appeased into indifference rather than terrorized into it."@en ;
    .

tgs:PanelDiscussion.RR_8_TheIndicMind a thinkr:Episode, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:createdDate "2026-08-13"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:updatedDate "2026-08-13"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.PanelDiscussion ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeSegment tgs:EpisodeSegment.RealityRoundtable ;
    skos:prefLabel "The Indic Mind: An Approach to the Metacrisis"@en ;
    skos:definition "Nate is joined by three Indian NGO leaders he met in person at a metacrisis symposium in Auroville less than 3 months before recording — Mohit Trivedi and Abhishek Thakore (co-founders, 2069 Ecosystem) and Kejal Savla (co-founder/CEO, Wisdom Tree) — to explore what they call the 'Indic mind': a distinctly non-Western, relationality-first, paradox-holding approach to the metacrisis, grounded in India's continuous spiritual and philosophical traditions rather than primarily technical or policy responses."@en ;
    dct:created "2024-04-25"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2024-05-12"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.EnergySystems, tgs:Subject.EconomicsAndMonetary ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    thinkr:hasGuest tgs:Persona.MohitTrivedi, tgs:Persona.AbhishekThakore, tgs:Persona.KejalSavla ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/episode/reality-roundtable-8> ;
    thinkr:hasTranscript <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2024/05/RR08TrivediThakoreSavlaTranscript.pdf> ;
    thinkr:discusses tgs:Concept.Superorganism ;
    skos:scopeNote "The Superorganism/Moloch concept is directly translated cross-culturally in this transcript — Mohit Trivedi renders it in his own language as 'Shaitan,' a real, notable moment of conceptual bridge-building this graph's own English-language Concept definition doesn't otherwise capture. Real, direct prior professional connection: Kejal Savla served as Chief Functionary of Abhishek Thakore's Blue Ribbon Movement for years before co-founding Wisdom Tree — confirmed via independent search, not asserted in the transcript itself."@en ;
    thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary "Distinctly different register from most other built RRs — philosophical and spiritual rather than technical, with real harmony among the three guests, who have spent years deliberately building what they call a 'relational field' with each other before ever discussing the metacrisis directly. The central original contribution is the 'Indic mind' itself, developed across the episode as a paradox-holding, relationality-first orientation distinct from Western crisis-response framing — Abhishek's own account of first meeting Nate is telling: they spent four hours talking about relationships and process without once mentioning climate or the metacrisis. The clearest moment of Nate's own assumption being corrected live comes when he directly states his expectation that Indians would feel resentment toward the Global North for disproportionate carbon emissions and climate impact — Abhishek and Mohit both describe something more layered: real awareness of the injustice without it curdling into blame, attributed to a cultural orientation toward self-directed agency rather than externalized grievance. Mohit's own account of living without air conditioning through 37-40°C heat by personal choice — while explicitly declining to 'glorify' the choice or claim it as a virtue — grounds the episode's more abstract philosophical claims in a concrete, lived practice. The one real point of divergence, held without resolution rather than smoothed into agreement, is each guest's relationship to hope: Kejal insists hope 'is not a choice... a stand that you take,' while Mohit says plainly 'hope is [an] illusioned word' and offers no answer for it, preferring confrontation with and embrace of the unknown instead. The episode's most emotionally exposed moment is Abhishek's own closing disclosure — 'it makes me very emotional to be honest' — describing his felt sense of a worldwide, unnamed network of people quietly doing this work, a claim he explicitly frames as testimony rather than argument."@en ;
    .

tgs:PanelDiscussion.RR_9_TheNextGenerationsDilemma a thinkr:Episode, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:createdDate "2026-08-13"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:updatedDate "2026-08-13"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.PanelDiscussion ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeSegment tgs:EpisodeSegment.RealityRoundtable ;
    skos:prefLabel "The Next Generation's Dilemma: Confronting the Metacrisis"@en ;
    skos:definition "Nate brings together three people in their early twenties — Priscilla Trịnh (his own former student and teaching assistant), James Branagan (Irish writer studying regenerative agriculture in Spain), and Natasha Linhart (ISEOF research/program associate) — to explore how younger people are personally metabolizing the metacrisis: their own awareness-grief-acceptance journeys, intergenerational blame and collaboration, food and water as central concerns, and the concrete practices each has adopted in daily life."@en ;
    dct:created "2024-05-08"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2024-06-09"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.EnergySystems, tgs:Subject.EconomicsAndMonetary ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    thinkr:hasGuest tgs:Persona.PriscillaTrinh, tgs:Persona.JamesBranagan, tgs:Persona.NatashaLinhart ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/episode/reality-roundtable-9> ;
    thinkr:hasTranscript <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2024/06/RR09BranaganLinhartTrinhTranscript.pdf> ;
    thinkr:discusses tgs:Concept.Superorganism, tgs:Concept.DistributedAgency ;
    skos:scopeNote "This episode's own real page shows an internal date inconsistency worth flagging plainly: 'issued' is listed as June 9, 2024, but 'Recorded on' is listed as May 8, 2025 — chronologically impossible (a recording date after its own issue date). Given the issued date matches the surrounding episode-number sequence exactly (RR-8 issued May 2024, RR-9 issued June 2024) and Nate's own real Substack post about this episode is dated June 9, 2024 and opens 'Yesterday, I hosted a Reality Roundtable,' the recorded date is treated here as 2024-05-08 (year corrected from the site's apparent typo), not the literal 2025 shown on the page. James Branagan's own real Substack essay ('Recalibrate Your Baseline') independently and directly uses the Superorganism/profit-maximization framing this episode's own discusses link points to."@en ;
    thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary "Distinctly personal and emotionally vulnerable, a real departure in register from most other built RRs — three young guests speaking candidly about their own psychological process of confronting the metacrisis rather than analyzing it from professional distance. Natasha Linhart's account is the most exposed: a real, named 'awareness → grief → acceptance' sequence including explicit disclosure of crying alone and feeling powerless during the grief phase, offered without being smoothed over ('that period really sucked. I don't want to sugarcoat it'). Priscilla Trịnh offers a genuinely different phenomenological texture for a similar process — 'a happy tumble down a hill,' exhilarating rather than purely painful — not a contradiction of Natasha's account so much as a different lived experience of a comparable arc, grounded in her own family history as children of Vietnamese refugees and an intersectional-feminist analytical lens distinct from the other two guests'. The one moment of real, direct pushback against Nate's own question comes from James Branagan, who states plainly that he doesn't observe the intergenerational blame Nate's framing assumed: 'I don't really see it... I don't even think blaming is productive' — reframing the real tension instead as a resource-versus-energy mismatch between generations. A genuine, unresolved difference in practice surfaces around social media: Natasha describes actively leaving it, while Priscilla stays engaged but holds herself to a personal rule against 'clicktivism' and 'rage-farming' — two different relationships to the same tool, not reconciled into one answer. The episode closes on a real continuity detail: Priscilla reveals that after Nate stopped teaching his University of Minnesota course, she and fellow students revived it as a peer-led seminar under a new name, 'What to Do About a Fucked Up World.'"@en ;
    .

tgs:PanelDiscussion.RR_10_TheEcologyOfCommunication a thinkr:Episode, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:createdDate "2026-08-13"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:updatedDate "2026-08-13"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.PanelDiscussion ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeSegment tgs:EpisodeSegment.RealityRoundtable ;
    skos:prefLabel "The Ecology of Communication: Moving Beyond Polarization in Service of Life"@en ;
    skos:definition "Nate is joined by four friends and repeat guests — Nora Bateson, Rex Weyler, Vanessa Andreotti, and Daniel Schmachtenberger — to examine communication itself as an ecology rather than a set of facts to transmit: how performative, monocultural, industrial-style language traps people in polarization, and what it might look like to instead seek understanding before being understood, start from commonality rather than the most contested point, and treat silence, context, and not-knowing as real parts of a genuine conversation."@en ;
    dct:created "2024-06-14"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2024-07-28"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.EnergySystems, tgs:Subject.EconomicsAndMonetary ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    thinkr:hasGuest tgs:Persona.NoraBateson, tgs:Persona.RexWeyler, tgs:Persona.VanessaAndreotti, tgs:Persona.DanielSchmachtenberger ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/episode/reality-roundtable-10> ;
    thinkr:discusses tgs:Concept.Schismogenesis, tgs:Concept.Metacrisis, tgs:SchoolOfThought.WarmData ;
    skos:scopeNote "2026-08-13: fully enriched from a complete real transcript + real linked show notes (MJSullivan's own upload), resolving the RR-10 gap flagged since Nora's original RR-26 bootstrap and closing the last remaining built-RR summary gap. Real, further citable material found but NOT built in this pass: Vanessa Andreotti's own real prior solo TGS appearance (#125, 'Hospicing Modernity'); the Dunning-Kruger curve, discussed at length by Schmachtenberger, has no matching Concept individual in this graph yet; Chief Ninawa (a real Indigenous leader quoted via Andreotti) and David Bohm/Krishnamurti (cited directly by Schmachtenberger) are real, deferred citation candidates."@en ;
    thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary "The most philosophically dense and least topic-driven of the built RRs — four guests who know each other's work well (Nate's own framing: 'you have been on my podcast, many of you more than once') build directly on each other rather than staying in their own lanes, so the discussion reads more like one extended, co-authored argument than four separate positions. Nora opens with the central frame (communication itself as ecology, not transmission); Rex immediately extends it with a direct ecological parallel (a monoculture forest versus a real one); Vanessa reframes it again through a decolonial lens (the difference between 'environment' as something external and 'ecology' as something we're inside of, and how English itself can fail to carry non-Western concepts of land); Daniel closes the opening round by grounding it further in physics and boundary theory (there are no truly separable things, only permeable exchange). None of the four ever pushes back on another's framing — disagreement essentially doesn't happen — but there is real, substantive addition happening in each turn, not just restatement. The one moment of process friction is instructive rather than adversarial: Daniel is candid that dialectical and empathetic communication tools can both be genuinely healing AND weaponized (a fact he treats as unresolved and requiring ongoing discernment, not a settled question), and he shares his own experience running couples counseling as the clearest illustration of how two people's honest accounts of the same relationship can diverge completely. Nate's own contribution is again unusually personal — he opens by admitting he was 'in a foul mood' before the conversation started and closes by directly stating his mood had genuinely lifted by the end, treating his own emotional shift as real evidence for the argument being made rather than an aside."@en ;
    .

tgs:PanelDiscussion.RR_11_TalesFromTheCarbonPulse a thinkr:Episode, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:createdDate "2026-08-13"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:updatedDate "2026-08-13"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.PanelDiscussion ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeSegment tgs:EpisodeSegment.RealityRoundtable ;
    skos:prefLabel "Tales from the Carbon Pulse: Tom Murphy, DJ White"@en ;
    skos:definition "Nate is joined by two decades-long friends and intellectual collaborators — physicist Tom Murphy and Greenpeace/Earthtrust co-founder DJ White — for a wide-ranging examination of the ecological cost of the carbon pulse: the collapse of wild mammal biomass (from 50,000kg per person 10,000 years ago to 2.5kg today), human-built mass now exceeding all living biomass on Earth, the physical limits of 'rebuildable' energy, and two genuinely different theories — Nate's supply-side view versus Tom's newly-considered demographic-collapse view — of when and why global human power output will peak."@en ;
    dct:created "2024-08-06"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2024-10-06"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.EnergySystems, tgs:Subject.EconomicsAndMonetary ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    thinkr:hasGuest tgs:Persona.TomMurphy, tgs:Persona.DJWhite ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/episode/reality-roundtable-11> ;
    thinkr:hasTranscript <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2024/10/RR11TranscriptTalesfromtheCarbonPulse.pdf> ;
    thinkr:discusses tgs:Concept.CarbonPulse, tgs:Concept.Superorganism ;
    dct:references tgs:Work.RealityBlind, tgs:Work.BottlenecksOf21stCentury, tgs:PanelDiscussion.RR_24_TheFantasyOfSpaceColonization, tgs:Persona.DennisMeadows ;
    skos:scopeNote "This episode's own real show notes directly cite both tgs:Work.RealityBlind and tgs:Work.BottlenecksOf21stCentury via the same reader-platform pattern, prompting this session's build of the latter as a real Work individual — resolving the second of DJ White's real 'three books' with Nate (a third remains unidentified; this episode's own transcript independently reconfirms the count of three without naming it, the same unresolved detail flagged during the RR-24 and RR-9 builds)."@en ;
    thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary "An especially close, long-standing friendship — all three explicitly reference exchanging 'thousands of emails over the last 10 years' — but not one that suppresses real disagreement. The clearest substantive divergence: Tom Murphy proposes, newly and tentatively, that collapsing global fertility rates could drive 'peak civilizational power' as early as 2033 through demand-side contraction rather than supply-side resource limits — Nate directly pushes back ('I'm going to push back on that... I think that's going to be a much bigger constraint... than the demographics'), and DJ White, while finding Murphy's data 'a head spinner,' states plainly he still comes down on the side of supply constraints. None of the three resolve this into agreement; it's held open. A real, notable moment of live intellectual honesty: Murphy explicitly describes changing his own mind mid-conversation, calling the demographic argument something he'd 'never considered before' and being 'gobsmacked' by the fertility data. DJ White's own account of he and his wife's decision not to have more biological children — framed provocatively as 'DNA is essentially our enemy at this point' — is offered candidly as central to his own philosophy, not softened. White also introduces a real, distinctive naming device mid-conversation: naming the hypothetical trillionth human being 'Naya' as a rhetorical anchor for thinking in deep time about population ethics. Murphy's own disillusionment with academia is disclosed personally and specifically — recounting 'blank stares' from colleagues he considered geniuses when he tried to discuss civilizational limits, and citing this disappointment as a real factor in his decision to retire at 53. The recurring 'man rushing at you with a hammer' analogy, explicitly credited to Dennis Meadows, becomes the episode's central reframe of the renewable-energy debate — arguing the relevant question isn't which weapon (fossil vs. renewable) but why there's an attacker at all."@en ;
    .

tgs:PanelDiscussion.RR_12_TheGlobalStakesOfTheUSElection a thinkr:Episode, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:createdDate "2026-08-13"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:updatedDate "2026-08-13"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.PanelDiscussion ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeSegment tgs:EpisodeSegment.RealityRoundtable ;
    skos:prefLabel "The Global Stakes of the U.S. Election: Debt, The Dollar, and Military Power"@en ;
    skos:definition "Recorded three weeks before the 2024 US election, Nate is joined by two returning financial analysts — Rabobank strategist Michael Every and Forest for the Trees founder Luke Gromen — for a detailed real-time argument about how the US resolves its debt/entitlement math (both agree: by 'printing the interest'), and what that implies for the dollar's reserve status, mercantilism, gold, and energy. A planned third guest, Helen Thompson, was unable to attend."@en ;
    dct:created "2024-10-16"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2024-10-27"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.EnergySystems, tgs:Subject.EconomicsAndMonetary ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    thinkr:hasGuest tgs:Persona.MichaelEvery, tgs:Persona.LukeGromen ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/episode/reality-roundtable-12> ;
    thinkr:hasTranscript <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2024/10/RR12TranscriptEveryGromen.docx.pdf> ;
    skos:scopeNote "Real, further citable material found but NOT built in this pass: Helen Thompson, the planned third guest, has her own real prior TGS episode (confirmed via this episode's own real show notes); Michael Every's own directly authored paper, 'Bretton Woods 3 Won't Work,' is hosted directly on thegreatsimplification.com's own domain (not a third-party link) — a real, distinctive primary source worth a future Work individual. Luke Gromen's own real prior solo TGS episode is confirmed via this episode's real show notes but not built out."@en ;
    thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary "Both guests name their own dynamic explicitly at the outset — 'we agree on about 90 percent... where we disagree is in what we think some of the short term, medium term and long term outcomes might be' — and the conversation lives up to that self-description: substantive, technical, real-time disagreement between two people who like and respect each other, not a debate performance. The central, sustained divergence is Gromen's case for gold re-emerging as a neutral reserve asset tied to energy value (his own vivid example: a wife's 8,000-pound truck becoming uneconomical once gasoline has to be paid for in finite, appreciating gold) against Every's case that fiat survives via targeted, directed credit and capital controls rather than a hard-money return — neither concedes the point, and Every explicitly names the shape of their disagreement himself near the close: 'it's like a slightly Rorschach... you're seeing one thing in it, I'm seeing something similar, but a bit different.' The one genuinely unexpected personal disclosure is Every's own account of being raised in a Marxist household in Europe, offered candidly as real biographical context for his read on China, not as a rhetorical move. A recurring shared device — the 'WWJD' bracelet, tied back to economist Joseph Schumpeter's own late-life religious turn — becomes their common ground for arguing that no monetary system, gold-backed or fiat, works without an underlying shared sense of what the economy is actually for. Nate's own framing of the roundtable format itself as a real Overton-window mechanism is worth noting directly: he suggests ideas normalized in conversations like this one may filter into real policy discourse, a claim Every's own professional paper (independently confirmed as hosted on TGS's own site) lends some real weight to."@en ;
    .

tgs:PanelDiscussion.RR_13_FutureCouncil a thinkr:Episode, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:createdDate "2026-08-13"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:updatedDate "2026-08-13"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.PanelDiscussion ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeSegment tgs:EpisodeSegment.RealityRoundtable ;
    skos:prefLabel "Future Council: How Children are Responding to our Planetary Crises"@en ;
    skos:definition "Nate first briefly interviews documentarian Damon Gameau, then is joined by three of the eight young activists (ages 11-14) featured in Gameau's film 'Future Council' — Skye Neville (North Wales), Clemence 'CC' Currie (Scotland), and Joaquin Minana (Netherlands) — about their real experiences confronting corporate leaders (Nestlé, Decathlon, ING) directly about climate accountability, and their own reflections on 'Groth,' the film's animated antagonist character explicitly built on Nate's own real Economic Superorganism concept."@en ;
    dct:created "2024-09-12"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2024-11-24"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.EnergySystems, tgs:Subject.EconomicsAndMonetary ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    thinkr:hasGuest tgs:Persona.DamonGameau, tgs:Persona.SkyeNeville, tgs:Persona.ClemenceCurrie, tgs:Persona.JoaquinMinana ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/episode/reality-roundtable-13> ;
    thinkr:hasTranscript <https://youtu.be/gi5VXVc2N6c> ;
    thinkr:discusses tgs:Concept.Superorganism ;
    dct:references tgs:Work.FutureCouncilFilm ;
    skos:scopeNote "This is the only episode in this graph sourced from a cleaned-up YouTube auto-generated transcript rather than an official TGS-hosted PDF (the site hosts no downloadable transcript for this episode) — treated with somewhat more caution regarding exact wording, though the substance is clear. This episode's own real show notes independently confirm the direct link between the film's 'Groth' character and Nate's own real Economic Superorganism paper. Three of the eight children featured in the film appear as guests; the other five are not covered in this pass. Personal-life details incidental to the interview (unrelated to the guests' environmental advocacy work) are omitted from this summary per this graph's general practice of scoping content to what is substantively and publicly relevant."@en ;
    thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary "A genuinely distinct episode in tone and format — Nate first interviews adult director Damon Gameau briefly before three young activists join. Gameau's own central intellectual contribution is his 'Three Hats' framework: that people (including the corporate leaders he filmed) are simultaneously economic actors, individuals seeking a good life, and aspiring good ancestors, and that current economic structures make it nearly impossible to hold all three at once — a real, substantive explanation for why even caring executives feel trapped. He discloses candidly that the filmmaking process personally 'cracked him open' emotionally, his most profound experience as a director. The three young guests offer real, differentiated (not uniform) assessments of their actual corporate meetings: they describe Decathlon's response as genuinely responsive (one of their suggestions — multiple name-tag spaces on clothing for reuse — was reportedly put into practice), in direct contrast to Nestlé and ING, which they describe as evasive when pressed on fossil-fuel-to-renewable transition plans. Their own economic reasoning is notably sophisticated for their age: a direct 'drug dealer' analogy (framing Exxon and Shell as suppliers who shift blame onto addicted consumers) and a separate 'cookie' analogy illustrating how blame gets redirected from the powerful onto the vulnerable. Skye Neville discloses a real, unresolved personal tension rather than claiming false consistency: extensive international travel for the film's promotion directly conflicts with her own environmental values, which she addresses through concrete but self-acknowledged imperfect offsetting (50 consecutive beach cleanups, tree planting) rather than denial of the contradiction. Nate's own closing philosophical framing — describing modern flight as 'indistinguishable from magic,' powered by dense ancient energy most people take entirely for granted — is offered as a direct extension of the conversation rather than a scripted transition. The episode closes with practical, peer-to-peer advice from the children themselves on managing climate anxiety: taking small concrete actions, allowing themselves to grieve openly rather than suppressing distress, and resisting the pressure to personally solve the crisis alone."@en ;
    .

tgs:PanelDiscussion.RR_14_TheFutureIsLocal a thinkr:Episode, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:createdDate "2026-08-13"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:updatedDate "2026-08-13"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.PanelDiscussion ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeSegment tgs:EpisodeSegment.RealityRoundtable ;
    skos:prefLabel "The Future is Local: Bioregioning 101"@en ;
    skos:definition "Nate hosts an introductory deep-dive on bioregioning with three practitioners — Daniel Christian Wahl (returning guest, author of 'Designing Regenerative Cultures'), Isabel Carlisle (Bioregional Learning Centre, South Devon, UK), and Samantha Power (BioFi Project, author of 'Bioregional Financing Facilities') — covering the concept's deep history, indigenous roots, how bioregional boundaries are actually drawn, and how finance, governance, and ecology combine in real projects already underway across multiple continents. Nate opens by directly admitting he 'knew next to nothing on bioregioning' before this recording."@en ;
    dct:created "2024-12-11"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2025-01-19"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.EnergySystems, tgs:Subject.EconomicsAndMonetary ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    thinkr:hasGuest tgs:Persona.DanielChristianWahl, tgs:Persona.IsabelCarlisle, tgs:Persona.SamanthaPower ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/episode/reality-roundtable-14> ;
    thinkr:hasTranscript <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/01/RR14Bioregioning101Transcript.pdf> ;
    thinkr:discusses tgs:Concept.Superorganism ;
    dct:references tgs:Persona.NoraBateson ;
    skos:scopeNote "Worth flagging plainly rather than silently accepting: the source transcript appears to mislabel several passages roughly between 47 and 60 minutes as 'Isabel Carlisle' whose content — first-person references to 'the BioFi project,' capital structuring, 'our theory of change,' finance-specific expertise — clearly matches Samantha Power's stated domain (confirmed by this episode's own real show notes, e.g. the 56:47 link to Samantha Power's own Medium article on 'nature states'), not Isabel's archaeology/place-based background. Treated here as a real transcription artifact, not corrected retroactively in the transcript itself, but not repeated as fact in this summary either. Real, further citable material found but NOT built in this pass: Daniel Christian Wahl's own real prior TGS solo episode; Atossa Soltani, described in the transcript as 'our mutual friend' of Nate and Samantha, now built (2026-09-02), real and confirmed via this episode's own show notes but not built as a Persona in this pass. Fritjof Capra, also directly name-checked here with his own real prior TGS episode, is no longer deferred — now built as a real Persona (see tgs:Persona.FritjofCapra). Bill Plotkin, also directly name-checked here with his own real prior TGS episode, is no longer deferred — now built as a real Persona (see tgs:Persona.BillPlotkin)."@en ;
    thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary "Framed explicitly by Nate as '101' for himself as much as the audience — he opens by admitting he 'knew next to nothing on bioregioning' before this recording, and the episode maintains that genuinely introductory, low-friction register throughout, with Wahl, Carlisle, and Power building one shared framework together rather than debating. The three guests' expertise divides cleanly and complementarily: Carlisle grounds the conversation in place and deep history (including her own vivid personal anecdote from her earlier archaeology career, recovering a woolly mammoth tusk at a 250,000-year-old Neanderthal site in Jersey), Wahl supplies the philosophical and historical framing (regenerative design, indigenous land-management history, a direct real citation of John Wesley Powell's 1879 proposal to organize the US by watershed boundaries), and Power supplies the financial and governance architecture via her own book's 'four returns' framework. The one real moment of internal critical challenge, rather than mutual reinforcement, comes from Wahl directly to Power: a pointed concern about whether bioregional finance risks becoming 'a backdoor for the finance industry to still be extractive' if patient capital is allowed to expect conventional financial returns — Power responds with a structured, real answer grounded in her book's own stated design principles (purpose-aligned funders, returns as a means rather than an end) rather than deflecting the concern. Wahl closes the episode by reciting a Gary Snyder poem in full in response to Nate's closing question about inevitability — the content of the poem itself is not reproduced here per this graph's own copyright discipline, only the fact of the recitation. The episode ends on a concrete, real organizing detail: plans are underway for a North American Bioregional Congress in 2026, the first since 2009."@en ;
    .

tgs:PanelDiscussion.RR_15_ThePlasticCrisis a thinkr:Episode, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:createdDate "2026-08-13"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:updatedDate "2026-08-13"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.PanelDiscussion ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeSegment tgs:EpisodeSegment.RealityRoundtable ;
    skos:prefLabel "The Plastic Crisis: A Health and Environmental Emergency"@en ;
    skos:definition "Nate is joined by three plastics researchers/advocates — pediatrician Leo Trasande (NYU), toxicologist Linda Birnbaum (former NIEHS/NTP director), and campaigner Christina Dixon (Environmental Investigation Agency, UN Global Plastics Treaty negotiations) — for a detailed examination of plastic's human-health impacts (endocrine disruption, cardiovascular disease, fertility, cancer), the regulatory 'innocent until proven guilty' status quo, the failure of recycling as a solution, and the state of the ongoing UN plastics treaty process, including a first-hand account of the Busan negotiations."@en ;
    dct:created "2025-01-21"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2025-02-10"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.EnergySystems, tgs:Subject.EconomicsAndMonetary ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    thinkr:hasGuest tgs:Persona.LeoTrasande, tgs:Persona.LindaBirnbaum, tgs:Persona.ChristinaDixon ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/episode/reality-roundtable-15> ;
    thinkr:hasTranscript <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/02/RR15TranscriptPlasticCrisis.pdf> ;
    thinkr:discusses tgs:Concept.CarbonPulse ;
    dct:references tgs:PanelDiscussion.RR_23_ThePlasticDetox, tgs:Persona.ShannaSwan, tgs:Persona.SianSutherland ;
    skos:scopeNote "This episode was already anticipated as a real, deferred reference during this graph's own RR-23 build ('Reality Roundtable #15, The Plastic Crisis') — that link is now real and bidirectional. This episode's own real show notes explicitly flag a factual correction: 'Nate misspoke this statistic in the episode' regarding hourly global plastic-waste figures — the transcript's spoken numbers (5.7 million toothpaste tubes, 570,000 cell phones, 2.3 million pairs of shoes) are corrected by the source itself to roughly 20 billion toothpaste tubes, 20 billion shoes, and 5 billion cell phones yearly; the error is preserved here as fact-about-the-recording, not repeated as a true statistic. Real, further citable material found but NOT built in this pass: Jane Muncke, Martin Scheringer, and Jeremy Grantham are all named directly in this episode's intro as prior TGS plastics guests, with real confirmed episode links, but are not yet built as Persona individuals in this graph — a prior draft of this graph incorrectly claimed Grantham already existed here, corrected now. Pete Myers is directly named by Birnbaum as a real personal connection to Nate ('my friend Pete Meyers'), now built (2026-09-02) — not built as a Persona in this pass."@en ;
    thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary "Deeply collegial and values-aligned throughout — Nate himself names the real dissonance directly: 'off camera... we were laughing and joking... and it's fricking disgusting.' The episode opens with a real intellectual move worth distinguishing from simple agreement: asked whether they agree with Jeremy Grantham's claim (made in a separate real TGS episode) that plastics/EDCs pose a bigger threat than climate change, Birnbaum partially concurs, but Dixon and Trasande both immediately reject the framing itself — arguing plastics and climate are inseparable, not competing threats, since plastics are fossil fuels and their production directly undermines any 1.5°C pathway. That reframing, not the original yes/no question, becomes the episode's real throughline. Nate's own CPAP mask is used as a recurring, genuinely personal anchor for his anxiety about untestable everyday plastic exposure. The clearest moment of real, extended speculation is Trasande's direct comparison to The Handmaid's Tale, predicting future conflict not simply over food and water but specifically over safe, uncontaminated food and water — Nate responds candidly that he'd 'never thought of that before,' a real live reaction rather than a rehearsed follow-up. Dixon deliberately pivots the conversation's tone using the Montreal Protocol as a real model of policy success, naming the shift herself ('I felt like we did go a bit doom mongering there for a second'). The episode's most vivid first-hand account is Trasande's own description of being the last public speaker at the Busan plastics-treaty negotiations at 2:30am, with Dixon revealing she stayed in the room specifically to support him. Nate's closing reframe of Dixon's self-deprecating 'I wish I was rich' comment — 'you are already rich, given what you're doing with your life' — is a small, values-driven correction offered warmly rather than argued."@en ;
    .

tgs:PanelDiscussion.RR_16_TheMadScrambleForPower a thinkr:Episode, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:createdDate "2026-08-13"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:updatedDate "2026-08-13"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.PanelDiscussion ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeSegment tgs:EpisodeSegment.RealityRoundtable ;
    skos:prefLabel "The Mad Scramble for Power: Global Superpowers' Strategies for Energy, Economics, and War"@en ;
    skos:definition "Nate is joined by returning guests Art Berman (petroleum geologist) and Michael Every (Rabobank global strategist), alongside new guest Izabella Kaminska (founder, The Blind Spot; senior finance editor, Politico Europe), for a wide-ranging real-time analysis of how energy, deindustrialization, military spending, bond markets, and AI/quantum computing are converging into what Michael Every frames as a civilizational shift from 'childlike' to 'adult' geopolitical thinking — closing on what open societies risk if that shift fails."@en ;
    dct:created "2025-03-10"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2025-03-24"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.EnergySystems, tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior, tgs:Subject.EconomicsAndMonetary, tgs:Subject.Geopolitics ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    thinkr:hasGuest tgs:Persona.ArtBerman, tgs:Persona.MichaelEvery, tgs:Persona.IzabellaKaminska ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/episode/reality-roundtable-16> ;
    thinkr:hasTranscript <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/04/RR16TranscriptTheMadScrambleforPower.pdf> ;
    dct:references tgs:PanelDiscussion.RR_22_CouldTheWestLoseTheResourceWars ;
    skos:scopeNote "Michael Every's own 'statecraft'/'what is GDP for' framing, directly and extensively discussed here, is the same real conceptual throughline he brings to RR-22 (built earlier this session with Craig Tindale) — a real, recurring cross-episode theme for this Persona specifically, not yet minted as its own Concept individual in this graph. This episode's own transcript independently reconfirms Every's real Marxist-household upbringing, first disclosed during the RR-12 build. Note: the episode's own real page metadata mislabels its Apple Music link with RR-15's title ('The Plastic Crisis...') — a real site artifact, not corrected here, only noted."@en ;
    thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary "A sophisticated, largely convergent three-way analysis among established macro thinkers, organized around Michael Every's own Biblical framing — a civilizational shift from 'childlike' to 'adult' understanding of energy's centrality — which Kaminska and Berman both independently extend rather than contest ('geology is not fair,' Every's line, gets Berman's direct addition: 'geography isn't fair' either). The clearest moment of real, if gentle, divergence is Berman calling the fear of a Russian invasion of Europe 'a preposterous notion' — Every doesn't rebut this directly but offers a genuinely different interpretive frame, invoking Leo Strauss's theory that societies need a unifying 'noble lie' or bogeyman to function, leaving Nate visibly updating live ('I'd never thought about it that way'). Kaminska's own original conceptual contribution — 'mutual liberation syndrome,' her term for how each side of a polarized society sees itself as the one fighting authoritarianism — is offered as a real, named framework, not just commentary. Every's own account of learning 'face culture' through his in-laws (a vivid, personal anecdote about blame being physically and socially transferred to a child after his mother-in-law's flatulence) grounds an otherwise abstract geopolitical point about states' unwillingness to admit error. The episode's most sustained extended metaphor is Every's own, built around Battlestar Galactica (both the 1970s original and its 2000s reboot) and later The Wire and Top Boy, arguing that policymakers who have never experienced real zero-sum competition are now playing exactly that game at the highest levels without understanding its rules. All three converge, without qualification, on honesty from leadership as the single highest-value intervention available — though each frames the mechanism differently: Berman via small 'islands of coherence' of genuinely informed people, Kaminska via leaders directly admitting past policy failures, Every via urging policymakers to study realistic depictions of zero-sum power rather than idealized ones."@en ;
    .

tgs:PanelDiscussion.RR_17_MovingFromApathyToAction a thinkr:Episode, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:createdDate "2026-08-13"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:updatedDate "2026-08-13"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.PanelDiscussion ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeSegment tgs:EpisodeSegment.RealityRoundtable ;
    skos:prefLabel "Moving from Apathy to Action: How Facing Grief Can Help Us Navigate a World in Crisis"@en ;
    skos:definition "Nate is joined by deep-ecology co-founder John Seed and grief ritualist Skye Cielita Flor to explore the Work That Reconnects — the framework developed by John Seed with Joanna Macy, Arne Naess, and Pat Fleming beginning in 1985 — covering its spiral structure (gratitude, honoring pain, seeing with new eyes, going forth), the Council of All Beings ritual, and why embodied grief and gratitude practices are, in their view, a necessary complement to purely factual awareness of ecological crisis."@en ;
    dct:created "2025-05-21"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2025-07-09"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    thinkr:hasGuest tgs:Persona.JohnSeed, tgs:Persona.SkyeCielitaFlor ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/episode/reality-roundtable-17> ;
    thinkr:hasTranscript <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/07/RR-17-Transcript-Moving-from-Apathy-to-Action.pdf> ;
    thinkr:discusses tgs:Concept.DistributedAgency ;
    dct:references tgs:Persona.DanielSchmachtenberger, tgs:Work.ThinkingLikeAMountain ;
    skos:scopeNote "This episode's own real show notes link directly to Nate's own real PhD thesis and to a separate real video of Nate himself explaining Marvin Harris's Cultural Materialism — both genuine primary sources about Nate specifically, flagged here as real and deferred rather than built out further in this pass. Francis Weller's own real prior TGS episode (#167) is directly cross-referenced again, matching the same real episode number already flagged during the RR-27 build earlier this session. Real, further citable material found but NOT built in this pass: Joanna Macy (deceased, central to the entire episode as co-originator of the Work That Reconnects, cited extensively but never a TGS guest herself). Randy Hayes (Rainforest Action Network) and Josh Schrei (directly named by both guests and by Nate as someone he 'would have been a good one' to include) are now both built (2026-09-02) — Randy Hayes, in particular, is directly named by Nate as 'my friend,' a real, notable detail now captured in his own Human bio."@en ;
    thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary "Warm, spiritually direct, and unusually candid about Nate's own past skepticism — he opens by admitting that despite encountering Joanna Macy's work repeatedly between 2005 and 2020, 'it never resonated with me' until recently, a genuine disclosure of a changed position rather than a rhetorical setup. Seed and Flor hold a real teacher-student relationship (Flor explicitly introduces herself as 'a student of John'), but the conversation is not simply hierarchical — Flor develops her own distinct specialization (what she calls 'animist perception,' grounded in Stephen Harrod Buhner's plant-intelligence framework) that Seed openly defers to as outside his own primary focus. Seed's central intellectual contribution is a real evolutionary-biology argument that feelings predate and are more reliable than cognition, having been selected for across nearly all of evolutionary history, while cognition is 'a recent intruder.' Both guests offer real, vivid personal origin stories: Seed's account of a spontaneous 1979 direct action to protect what became Terania Creek rainforest, which he describes feeling like 'a mental breakdown' in the moment; Flor's account of witnessing an oil spill devastate an indigenous fishing community she was living with in the Peruvian Amazon, which directly redirected her work. A real moment of practical honesty rather than idealism: Seed recounts being asked to rebrand the Council of All Beings as corporate 'team building,' and reports it has failed every single time he tried — the ritual depends on authentic shared intention, not technique alone. Seed also discloses a genuinely humbling realization: after co-creating the Council of All Beings with Joanna Macy in 1985, he later witnessed Hopi practitioners performing what he recognized as the same ritual, which they told him they'd been doing for 10,000 years without interruption — a real moment of discovering rediscovery rather than invention. The episode closes on full, unqualified convergence between both guests on the same practical advice (find like-minded people close to home), which Nate notes directly echoes something said, coincidentally, on a completely unrelated podcast he recorded the same day."@en ;
    .

tgs:PanelDiscussion.RR_18_HowWaterShapesOurPlanet a thinkr:Episode, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:createdDate "2026-08-13"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:updatedDate "2026-08-13"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.PanelDiscussion ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeSegment tgs:EpisodeSegment.RealityRoundtable ;
    skos:prefLabel "How Water Shapes Our Planet: The Undervalued Resource that Supports Everything We Do"@en ;
    skos:definition "Nate is joined by three water experts operating at different scales — Heather Cooley (Pacific Institute, US water-energy policy), Mike Joy (New Zealand freshwater ecologist and pollution-policy critic), and Zach Weiss (Elemental Ecosystems/Water Stories, hands-on watershed restoration) — to examine the full hydrological cycle, the 'biotic pump' mechanism by which vegetation draws atmospheric moisture inland, agricultural and industrial water pollution, groundwater depletion, and both the policy and landscape-level interventions each guest sees as most effective."@en ;
    dct:created "2025-06-11"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2025-09-03"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.EcologyAndEnvironment ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    thinkr:hasGuest tgs:Persona.HeatherCooley, tgs:Persona.MikeJoy, tgs:Persona.ZachWeiss ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/episode/reality-roundtable-18> ;
    thinkr:hasTranscript <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/09/RR-18-Transcript-How-Water-Shapes-Our-Planet.pdf> ;
    skos:scopeNote "This is the final Reality Roundtable built in this graph, completing the full numbered sequence RR-1 through RR-27. This episode's own real show notes cite New Zealand's territorial governance based on watershed/catchment boundaries — a real, substantive thematic echo of the bioregional-governance ideas built at length in this graph's own RR-14, though not a direct citation within this transcript itself. Real, further citable material found but NOT built in this pass: John Kitzhaber's own real prior TGS episode (#70) and Roman Krznaric's (#142, citing Spain's historic Water Court) — both real, confirmed, deferred."@en ;
    thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary "Genuinely complementary across three distinct scales rather than competing framings: Cooley supplies US institutional/policy analysis (the water-energy nexus, federal underinvestment), Joy supplies pointed regulatory critique grounded in decades fighting New Zealand's own dairy-industry water pollution, and Weiss supplies hands-on, decentralized landscape restoration practice. Joy's own biography is genuinely unusual and disclosed candidly: he left school at 17, worked as a truck driver, sheep farmer, and New Zealand Security Intelligence Service agent before enrolling at university at 33 — a real, non-academic path into a career built on 'blunt commentary' that has drawn criticism from the dairy industry and a former Prime Minister by name. Weiss's own account of the 'biotic pump' — living ecosystems drawing atmospheric moisture inland from the oceans — includes a real moment of scientific humility rather than overclaiming: his own words describe '72 anomalies of water' that remain without full physiological or physical explanation. The clearest moment of real, pointed institutional criticism is Joy's account of New Zealand's practical non-enforcement of its own environmental rules, describing the effective 'penalty' for water-pollution noncompliance as negligible. Rather than converging on a single lever of change, all three hold their differing theories of intervention (policy reform, regulatory enforcement, decentralized restoration) as genuinely complementary rather than competing, closing on shared, concrete real-world success stories — sponge cities, spring restoration, and community-led decentralized water-retention projects — as evidence for cautious, grounded hope rather than abstract optimism."@en ;
    .

tgs:PanelDiscussion.RR_19_DarkTriad a thinkr:Episode, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:createdDate "2026-08-13"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:updatedDate "2026-08-13"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.PanelDiscussion ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeSegment tgs:EpisodeSegment.RealityRoundtable ;
    skos:prefLabel "Dark Triad Personality Traits: How Psychopathy, Narcissism, and Machiavellianism Impact Our Cultures & Social Systems"@en ;
    skos:definition "Nate is joined by psychoanalyst Dr. Nancy McWilliams and forensic psychologist Dr. Reid Meloy to examine the Dark Triad (psychopathy, narcissism, Machiavellianism) and Dark Tetrad (adding sadism) from complementary clinical and evolutionary angles — why these traits persist in the population despite their costs, how they manifest differently in individual relationships versus positions of power, and what real, non-naive guardrails (strong local community, truth-telling, functioning democratic institutions) might do to contain their worst effects at a civilizational scale."@en ;
    dct:created "2025-07-22"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2025-09-17"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    thinkr:hasGuest tgs:Persona.NancyMcWilliams, tgs:Persona.ReidMeloy ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/episode/reality-roundtable-19> ;
    thinkr:hasReplay <https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=XnHSRCN1iAY>, <https://open.spotify.com/episode/6Rg4noyHxfFpgy8ub6jvZH> ;
    thinkr:hasTranscript <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/09/RR-19-Transcript-Dark-Triad-Personality-Traits.pdf> ;
    thinkr:discusses tgs:Concept.DarkTriad, tgs:Concept.Superorganism ;
    thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary "Complementary rather than adversarial, but distinct in texture from the other RRs — Nancy McWilliams and Reid Meloy defer to and build on each other throughout rather than debate, yet each brings a genuinely different disciplinary lens rather than illustrative texture around a shared thesis. McWilliams opens by explicitly distancing her own approach from trait-based diagnosis ('I don't tend to think in terms of traits, I tend to think in terms of themes'), then supplies detailed clinical material Meloy doesn't have — how she actually works with psychopathic patients in therapy, her critique of the DSM's own diagnostic history. Meloy supplies forensic and evolutionary material she doesn't have — population prevalence estimates, predator/prey framing, extreme case data from his own casework. The one moment of honest gap rather than synthesis: when Nate raises Scott Barry Kaufman's 'Light Triad,' Meloy admits he isn't familiar with the term at all, and McWilliams is the one who has looked it up and explains it. Where the two genuinely converge, they amplify rather than merely agree — when McWilliams calls the entire history of the United States 'somewhat psychopathic,' Meloy doesn't qualify it, he extends it ('the theme of psychopathy has been woven through the history of the United States'). Nate's own contribution goes beyond interviewing: he draws a real cross-disciplinary connection between his own energy/power background and the psychological concept of power ('power in an ecology sense is an ecosystem... accessing an energy gradient per unit time'), and at the close, builds Meloy's closing metaphor (Dark Triad individuals as 'passengers on the train' who must never be allowed to drive) directly into his own recurring Superorganism framing, explicitly flagging he needs to think further about how the two connect rather than presenting it as settled."@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "2026-08-13: fully rebuilt from a complete real transcript + real fetched show notes (MJSullivan's own upload, cross-verified against a direct fetch of the episode's own page), replacing the earlier bare stub built from RR-26's own cross-reference alone. Real, further citable material found but NOT built into this graph in this pass: David Sloan Wilson (real, own TGS episode #56, confirmed via this episode's own real show notes) — a genuine further multi-level-selection/Superorganism connection, deliberately deferred rather than chased down unprompted. 'TGS Episodes 1 + 2' for Zak Stein in this episode's own show notes are ordinal labels for his 1st/2nd appearances (real URLs confirm #122/#180), not literal episode numbers — resolved via direct link inspection, not assumed."@en ;
    .

tgs:PanelDiscussion.RR_20_HackingHumanAttachment a thinkr:Episode, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:createdDate "2026-08-13"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:updatedDate "2026-08-13"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.PanelDiscussion ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeSegment tgs:EpisodeSegment.RealityRoundtable ;
    skos:prefLabel "Hacking Human Attachment: The Loneliness Crisis, Cognitive Atrophy, and Other Personal Dangers of AI"@en ;
    skos:definition "Nate is joined by returning guests Nora Bateson and Zak Stein to examine AI's effects at the individual, relational level rather than the usual economic/large-scale framing — how chatbots are designed to exploit attachment vulnerabilities, the early evidence of cognitive atrophy and eroded person-to-person connection this produces, and what a more embodied, deliberately human-to-human counterweight might look like."@en ;
    dct:created "2025-10-14"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2025-11-05"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    thinkr:hasGuest tgs:Persona.NoraBateson, tgs:Persona.ZakStein ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/episode/reality-roundtable-20> ;
    thinkr:hasTranscript <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/11/RR-20-Transcript-Hacking-Human-Attachment-1.pdf> ;
    thinkr:discusses tgs:Concept.CarbonPulse, tgs:Concept.DarkTriad, tgs:Concept.Superorganism ;
    thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary "A real division of labor between two friends who never disagree but consistently work from different registers — Zak Stein supplies the mechanistic explanation (mirror neurons, the attachment system versus the attentional system, Harlow's monkeys and operant conditioning, the historical throughline from B.F. Skinner to AI tutors), while Nora Bateson consistently reframes the same point in relational/felt terms (warm data, the missing 'rhythm of breath' in digital communication, the edge of the self). Neither treats this as competing frameworks; each explicitly builds on the other's point rather than substituting for it. The one moment resembling internal friction is Zak checking his own argument's edges rather than disagreeing with Nora — catching himself mid-critique to note that dismissing all technology outright is its own kind of naivety ('how far will you push your techno-pessimism'), and that the same critical tools used against chatbots could equally be pointed at real technological benefits (Novocaine, reliable garbage collection) he doesn't actually want to lose. Nate's own participation is unusually personal for an interview host — he narrates his own emotional arc in real time (a 'pit in my stomach' partway through, open shame about once mocking the Amish for opting out of exactly this risk, then explicit relief and hope by the close), and at one point the conversation is genuinely interrupted by an unplanned real-world moment — a garbage truck's noise triggering his own threat-detection reflex — which he and the guests fold directly into the discussion rather than treat as a distraction, since it illustrates the very embodied, evolved cognition the conversation argues chatbots are bypassing. The clearest moment of real-time concept-building: Nate offers 'wide boundary self-awareness' as his own gloss on Nora's father's old definition of sanity ('the ability to perceive your own epistemology'), and Nora accepts and extends it rather than correcting it."@en ;
    dct:references tgs:PanelDiscussion.RR_19_DarkTriad ;
    skos:scopeNote "Real, further citable material found via this episode's own real show notes but NOT built into this graph in this pass, deliberately deferred: Frankly #112 ('The Quadruple Bifurcation') and TGS #198 (Stacy Mitchell, antitrust) — both real cross-references, neither yet stubbed as an Episode individual, so no dct:references asserted for either. Real, well-evidenced NEW Concept candidates surfaced but not minted this pass: Attachment Theory (John Bowlby), Supernormal Stimuli (Niko Tinbergen — directly cited via Work.RealityBlind, already in this graph), Goodhart's Law (Charles Goodhart), and a real Persona candidate, Harry Harlow (of Harlow's monkeys, foundational attachment research) — all real, all independently verifiable, held for a follow-up pass rather than built unprompted."@en ;
    .

tgs:PanelDiscussion.RR_21_ArmsRaceOrTheHumanRace a thinkr:Episode, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:createdDate "2026-08-13"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:updatedDate "2026-08-13"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.PanelDiscussion ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeSegment tgs:EpisodeSegment.RealityRoundtable ;
    skos:prefLabel "Arms Race or the Human Race? Governance in the Age of AI, Nuclear Threats, and Geopolitical Brinkmanship"@en ;
    skos:definition "Nate is joined by geopolitical risk experts Mark Medish (former Clinton NSC/Treasury official) and Chuck Watson (Cold War-era intelligence/military background, now disaster-and-conflict modeling) to examine the erosion of international governance — starting from first-principles definitions of governance, government, and rule of law, then moving through nuclear arms control history to the real, imminent expiration of New START and what its lapse would mean, and closing on whether AI's entry into military decision-making removes the last human hesitation that has, so far, kept nuclear war from happening."@en ;
    dct:created "2026-01-08"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2026-01-21"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.Geopolitics ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    thinkr:hasGuest tgs:Persona.MarkMedish, tgs:Persona.ChuckWatson ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/episode/reality-roundtable-21> ;
    thinkr:hasTranscript <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/01/RR-21-Transcript-Why-Science-Communication-Fails.pdf> ;
    thinkr:discusses tgs:Concept.DarkTriad ;
    dct:references tgs:PanelDiscussion.RR_19_DarkTriad ;
    skos:scopeNote "Real, further citable material found but NOT built in this pass: Richard Gephardt has 2 real confirmed prior TGS episodes (#1 and #148) and is the direct source of the episode's own governance/violence framing ('politics is a substitute for violence') — not bootstrapped here. Anastassia Makarieva (biotic pump researcher, TGS #193) is directly referenced by Nate himself recalling a separate real episode. This episode's own real transcript-download filename is a genuine site artifact worth flagging honestly rather than silently correcting: it's titled 'RR-21-Transcript-Why-Science-Communication-Fails.pdf,' apparently a leftover/mismatched filename from an unrelated episode, even though the URL itself correctly resolves to this episode's real transcript content."@en ;
    thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary "Unlike several of the more adversarial-leaning built RRs, Medish and Watson are remarkably aligned throughout — this reads as two experts building one converging analysis together rather than staying in separate lanes or challenging each other. The real complementary split is disciplinary, not oppositional: Watson supplies the technical/military texture (missile specifics, game theory, his own direct Reagan-era technical postings), while Medish supplies the diplomatic/legal frame (treaty architecture, his own direct experience organizing Clinton-Putin summits). Neither ever pushes back on the other's claims; each mostly extends or adds precision to what the other just said. The one genuinely personal, unscripted moment is Medish's own disclosure that he was physically present, as an infant, at JFK's 1963 American University 'Strategy for Peace' speech — his father received his PhD diploma from Kennedy that same day — offered as lived, not abstract, connection to the arms-control history being discussed. The clearest point of real divergence isn't between the two guests but within each of their own closing answers to Nate's direct question about whether current leadership can rise to this moment: Medish frames himself as 'an optimist... but worried,' holding both at once; Watson draws a sharper line, affirming humanity broadly can meet the challenge while flatly denying that current political leaders specifically can ('can our leaders meet these challenges? ...my answer is no, they can't'), attributing this to a real structural mismatch between the personality traits that win elections and the self-doubting, revision-willing traits good decision-making actually requires."@en ;
    .

tgs:PanelDiscussion.RR_22_CouldTheWestLoseTheResourceWars a thinkr:Episode, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:createdDate "2026-08-13"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:updatedDate "2026-08-13"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.PanelDiscussion ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeSegment tgs:EpisodeSegment.RealityRoundtable ;
    skos:prefLabel "Could the West Lose the Resource Wars? AI, Rare Earths, and Economic Statecraft"@en ;
    skos:definition "Nate brings together two returning guests who had been independently telling parallel stories for years without knowing each other — investor/materials analyst Craig Tindale and Rabobank strategist Michael Every — to merge their respective frameworks on critical-materials scarcity, Chinese industrial dominance, and 'economic statecraft' into one account of why the West's China-versus-US rivalry is fundamentally a materials-and-manufacturing contest, not a purely financial or ideological one. Recorded live as the Iran conflict was actively breaking, and closing on whether AI itself is now the largest new driver of critical-materials demand."@en ;
    dct:created "2026-02-26"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2026-03-04"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.EconomicsAndMonetary, tgs:Subject.Geopolitics ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    thinkr:hasGuest tgs:Persona.CraigTindale, tgs:Persona.MichaelEvery ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/episode/reality-roundtable-22> ;
    thinkr:hasTranscript <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/03/RR-22-Transcript-Could-the-West-Lose-the-Resource-Wars_.pdf> ;
    thinkr:discusses tgs:Concept.GrowthImperative ;
    dct:references tgs:PanelDiscussion.RR_21_ArmsRaceOrTheHumanRace ;
    skos:scopeNote "Nate's own words frame this directly: 'probably one of the most intense conversations I've had on this platform in the four years of doing this podcast.' Real, further citable material found but NOT built in this pass: Craig's own prior TGS episode (#207); Michael's own extensive prior history (RR-16, RR-12, TGS #118) — see both Personas' own scopeNotes. Zak Stein is directly referenced again with his own real episodes (#180, #122, RR-20), all already built in this graph."@en ;
    thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary "Framed by Nate himself at the outset as two people who had been independently 'telling parallel stories' finally merging them — and the conversation lives up to that framing, with Tindale and Every remarkably aligned throughout rather than debating. The real complementary split is textural, not oppositional: Tindale supplies ground-level industrial/supply-chain detail (his own 'golden screw' concept — a single missing input that halts an entire system — and a two-ledger financial-versus-material framework), while Every supplies the macro geopolitical-strategy vocabulary ('economic statecraft,' pointed comparisons of US and Chinese industrial policy). Each consistently extends the other's point rather than qualifying it; Every at one point simply says 'I concur' before adding his own layer. The one moment either explicitly flags real disagreement is telling precisely because it's so rare: Every says outright 'this is the first time I differ from Craig at all,' pushing back on Tindale's skepticism that AI job losses will be modest — arguing that even a much smaller disruption (8%, not the touted 80%) could be politically destabilizing given how radicalized and polarized the public already is. Nate's own role is unusually reflective for an interview host — he calls this 'probably one of the most intense conversations' of the podcast's four-year run, and twice interrupts the material density with genuinely human asides (a girlfriend's text about adopting a puppy, arriving mid-Tindale's point about elder care). The episode closes not on analysis but on personal practice: Tindale describes growing and canning his own food as a direct, literal response to the very systemic fragility he spent the previous ninety minutes diagnosing."@en ;
    .

tgs:PanelDiscussion.RR_23_ThePlasticDetox a thinkr:Episode, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:createdDate "2026-08-13"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:updatedDate "2026-08-13"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.PanelDiscussion ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeSegment tgs:EpisodeSegment.RealityRoundtable ;
    skos:prefLabel "The Plastic Detox: Reducing Endocrine Disruptors for Better Fertility and Human Health"@en ;
    skos:definition "Nate reunites two returning guests and real-world colleagues — reproductive epidemiologist Shanna Swan and plastics-industry campaigner Sian Sutherland — around Swan's new Netflix documentary 'The Plastic Detox,' in which she runs a real 3-month endocrine-disrupting-chemical-reduction intervention with 6 couples facing unexplained infertility, tracking urinary chemical metabolites and sperm quality before and after simple household product swaps. The conversation moves from the documentary's specific findings to the broader case that plastics and endocrine disruption are the 'micro' version of the same fossil-fuel-driven story as climate change, and closes on Sutherland's Plastic Free Babies campaign targeting the first 1,000 days of life."@en ;
    dct:created "2026-02-19"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2026-03-18"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.EcologyAndEnvironment ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    thinkr:hasGuest tgs:Persona.ShannaSwan, tgs:Persona.SianSutherland ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/episode/reality-roundtable-23> ;
    thinkr:hasTranscript <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/03/RR-23-Transcript-The-Plastic-Detox.pdf> ;
    thinkr:discusses tgs:Concept.CarbonPulse, tgs:Concept.Superorganism ;
    dct:references tgs:PanelDiscussion.RR_15_ThePlasticCrisis ;
    skos:scopeNote "Shanna Swan's own real first-ever TGS appearance (#2, one of the podcast's earliest episodes) is no longer deferred — now built as a real Episode individual (tgs:Interview.TGS_2_ShannaSwan), with a real dct:references link back to this episode. Sian Sutherland's (#89) real, further citable material found but NOT built in this pass: solo episodes with Jane Muncke (#154, #104), Wes Carter (#186), Jeremy Grantham (#155, #99), and Martin Scheringer (#37, PFAS) — none built as Persona or Episode individuals in this pass; a prior version of this note incorrectly claimed Grantham was already built here, which was never actually verified and is corrected now. Reality Roundtable #15 ('The Plastic Crisis') is no longer deferred — now built as a real Episode individual, with a real dct:references link back to this episode."@en ;
    thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary "Warm and deeply collegial throughout — Swan and Sutherland are established real-world colleagues (co-present at UN Plastics Treaty negotiations) as well as friends, and Nate's own opening explicitly names the shift from 'meeting you two as activists and scientists' to being actual friends after four-plus years. The complementary split is expertise-based, not oppositional: Swan supplies the clinical/epidemiological data (urinary metabolites, sperm counts, the documentary's own specific intervention protocol), while Sutherland supplies industry and policy strategy (the 'cattle prod and lightning rod' framing for A Plastic Planet's work, the Plastic Free Babies campaign). One moment stands out as genuinely different from every other RR built this session: asked directly for the documentary's central result — how many of the six couples became pregnant — Swan deliberately declines to answer, wanting viewers to watch the film rather than get a spoiler, and Sutherland backs her ('no spoilers from Shanna'). This isn't evasion in the sense of avoiding a hard question; it's a real, distinct interactional dynamic — a promotional and narrative consideration overriding an otherwise-answered pattern of direct scientific replies throughout the rest of the episode. The other genuinely honest moment comes when Nate raises a deliberately uncomfortable question — whether awareness of these interventions could become an 'evolutionary filter' where only the informed and wealthy can afford to reduce their exposure and successfully reproduce — and Swan responds with real epistemic humility rather than a rehearsed answer: 'I think it sounds brilliant and I don't have any response.' The closest thing to a substantive correction comes from Nate himself at the close, gently but directly qualifying Sutherland's framing that 'we' collectively care about the environment: 'We do care about the environment. The system doesn't care' — offered as an addition to her point rather than a rebuttal, but a real distinction he clearly wanted on the record."@en ;
    .

tgs:PanelDiscussion.RR_24_TheFantasyOfSpaceColonization a thinkr:Episode, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:createdDate "2026-08-13"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:updatedDate "2026-08-13"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.PanelDiscussion ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeSegment tgs:EpisodeSegment.RealityRoundtable ;
    skos:prefLabel "The Fantasy of Space Colonization: The Spaceship We're Already On"@en ;
    skos:definition "Nate is rejoined by two ~20-year friends, astrophysicist Tom Murphy and eco-interventionist DJ White, to biophysically ground the resurgent techno-optimist enthusiasm for space mining and off-world colonization — the economic impossibility of asteroid mining, the physiological brutality of long-duration spaceflight, and the argument that space colonization functions as a convenient story letting humanity off the hook for damage being done at home. Closes by redirecting attention to Earth itself as the 'spaceship we're already on.'"@en ;
    dct:created "2026-02-24"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2026-04-15"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior, tgs:Subject.EcologyAndEnvironment ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    thinkr:hasGuest tgs:Persona.TomMurphy, tgs:Persona.DJWhite ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/episode/reality-roundtable-24> ;
    thinkr:hasTranscript <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/04/RR-24-Transcript-The-Fantasy-of-Space-Colonization.pdf> ;
    thinkr:discusses tgs:SchoolOfThought.TechnoOptimism, tgs:Concept.CarbonPulse, tgs:Concept.EnergyReturnOnInvestment ;
    dct:references tgs:Concept.GreatSimplification ;
    thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary "Even more uniformly convergent than RR-1 — Tom Murphy and DJ White, ~20-year friends, never actually disagree with each other anywhere in the transcript; they build on and extend each other's points throughout, arriving from two complementary but distinct disciplinary angles rather than debating one. Murphy supplies the hard biophysical case (asteroid-mining economics that work out to roughly $130,000/ounce for plain dirt; radiation doses that make a lifetime in space a near-guaranteed early cancer death; the rocket equation implying an asteroid-return mission would need several asteroids' worth of fuel), while White supplies the ecological/consciousness case (his own dolphin-cognition and Greenpeace background, arguing the 'real profound stuff' — minds we can already talk to and are actively driving extinct — is here on Earth, not hypothetically out there). Both independently and repeatedly frame techno-optimism as displaced religion (AI as new gods, uploading as immortality, Dyson spheres as cathedrals), not as a rival scientific claim worth adjudicating on the merits. The one place real friction appears is not between the two guests but against the absent, unnamed techno-optimist position itself, which Nate keeps them pointed at with pointed, sometimes deliberately provocative questions (invoking Matt Damon's potatoes in The Martian, asking directly why two of 'the smartest people' he knows hold a minority view). The episode closes on a register shift from technical to personal — White's story of comforting a dying baby humpback whale, Murphy's closing 'talk to animals' — both converging one final time on the same point: the wonder people project onto space is already available here, and is being destroyed."@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Built specifically as a test case for the 'antagonistic' side of this graph's own network, per MJSullivan's own framing — the episode's real content directly and repeatedly names techno-optimism as its target (Nate's own words: 'the resurgence of ambitions for space mining and space colonization amongst the leading techno optimist technologists of our age'), not inferred from tone. Real, further citable material found but NOT built in this pass: a possible THIRD Nate/DJ White co-authored book (Nate's own words call DJ 'co-writer of three books,' but only 2 are currently in this graph — Work.RealityBlind and 'The Bottlenecks of the 21st Century,' the latter itself not yet built as a Work individual either); TGS_18 (Tom Murphy's own solo episode), Frankly_22, and RR-11 ('Tales from the Carbon Pulse,' Tom+DJ together) — all real, all deferred."@en ;
    .

tgs:PanelDiscussion.RR_25_WhyStandardizedTestingContributedToTheMetacrisis a thinkr:Episode, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:createdDate "2026-08-13"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:updatedDate "2026-08-13"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.PanelDiscussion ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeSegment tgs:EpisodeSegment.RealityRoundtable ;
    skos:prefLabel "Why Standardized Testing Contributed to the Metacrisis – and How to Fix It"@en ;
    skos:definition "Nate is joined by developmental psychologist Theo Dawson (founder, Lectica) and returning guest Zak Stein — established real academic collaborators and co-founders who published together as far back as 2004 — to trace how 20th-century standardized testing (Army Alpha/Beta, the SAT, No Child Left Behind) narrowed education toward memorization and measurable-for-employers outcomes at the expense of what Dawson argues is real 'measurement' of mental development. The conversation moves from that historical account through Lectica's own real MindLog tool and closes on Zak Stein's argument, from his own paper 'Education is the Metacrisis,' that civilizational dysfunction is downstream of how a society raises its next generation."@en ;
    dct:created "2026-03-25"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2026-05-20"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    thinkr:hasGuest tgs:Persona.TheoDawson, tgs:Persona.ZakStein ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/episode/reality-roundtable-25> ;
    thinkr:hasTranscript <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/05/RR-25-Transcript-Learning-in-a-Way-that-Actually-Matters.pdf> ;
    thinkr:discusses tgs:Concept.Superorganism ;
    dct:references tgs:PanelDiscussion.RR_20_HackingHumanAttachment, tgs:Persona.NoraBateson, tgs:Persona.DanielSchmachtenberger ;
    skos:scopeNote "Recorded the same day as the real 2026 Iran war/Strait of Hormuz crisis (March 25, 2026), directly acknowledged by Nate at the top of the conversation. Real, further citable material found but NOT built in this pass: Jonathan Haidt's own real prior TGS episode (#59) on youth mental health; Iain McGilchrist, already a real, built Persona in this graph, is directly cross-referenced again via 3 of his own real prior TGS episodes (#217, #165, #85) for his point about measurement destroying original beauty — the same point Nate raises early in this transcript, itself referencing a real conversation he'd had with McGilchrist 'yesterday' relative to this recording. Both Nora Bateson and Daniel Schmachtenberger, already real and built in this graph, are directly cross-referenced again by name (Bateson via 'Warm Data,' Schmachtenberger via the term 'generative dynamic')."@en ;
    thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary "Dawson and Stein are not merely co-guests but established real academic collaborators — they co-published together as early as 2004, and Stein is himself a co-founder of Dawson's organization, Lectica. That history shows throughout: Stein consistently defers to Dawson on technical measurement detail while supplying the broader philosophical and civilizational framing, and at one point directly credits her as the origin of his own thinking ('the first part of my career was working with Theo'). The episode's central intellectual move is Dawson's own sharp distinction between 'testing' (what nearly all of 20th-century education actually did) and 'measurement' (a rarer, content-independent standard she argues her own Lectical Assessment System achieves) — a distinction she returns to repeatedly as the conversation's organizing frame. Nate draws an explicit bridge between the guests' education focus and his own usual energy/systems framing, asking Stein directly to connect Lectica's work to 'the broader meta-crisis' — Stein answers by invoking his own paper, 'Education is the Metacrisis,' and reframing civilizational purpose itself as a choice between accumulating abstract profit and regenerating the next generation's capacity. The one small, genuinely human moment is Stein's own self-deprecating aside about his dyslexia while trying to recall a historical name mid-sentence. Dawson's own most personal disclosure — living off-grid with her children for a significant stretch, and the jolt of re-encountering advertising and its effect on her self-image afterward — grounds the episode's abstract argument about embodied learning in a real, lived account rather than only theory. Nate closes by revealing the episode's own origin: a private, 1.5-hour conversation between him and Stein about AI and education that led directly to inviting Dawson onto the show."@en ;
    .

tgs:PanelDiscussion.RR_26_WhyCommunityFails a thinkr:Episode, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:createdDate "2026-08-13"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:updatedDate "2026-08-13"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.PanelDiscussion ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeSegment tgs:EpisodeSegment.RealityRoundtable ;
    skos:prefLabel "Why 'Community' Fails: Everyone Wants a Village, Nobody Wants to Be a Villager"@en ;
    skos:definition "Nate is joined by Nora Bateson (creator of Warm Data Labs) and her International Bateson Institute colleagues Jonathan Goldsmith and Lucas Jackson for a conversation about why so many attempts at community fail. Drawing on Bateson's concept of 'communing' as the necessary precursor to community, the group argues genuine human connection is being undermined by algorithmic fragmentation, scripted discourse, and transactional/individualist culture — and that the way out isn't a formula but older practices: mutual learning, radical hospitality, and relating outside one's perceived social role."@en ;
    dct:created "2026-04-22"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2026-06-10"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    thinkr:hasGuest tgs:Persona.NoraBateson, tgs:Persona.JonathanGoldsmith, tgs:Persona.LucasJackson ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/episode/reality-roundtable-26> ;
    dct:source <https://natehagens.substack.com/p/why-community-fails-everyone-wants> ;
    thinkr:discusses tgs:Concept.Communing, tgs:Concept.Symmathesy, tgs:Concept.Schismogenesis, tgs:SchoolOfThought.WarmData, tgs:Concept.Superorganism, tgs:Concept.Metacrisis, tgs:Concept.RadicalHospitality, tgs:Concept.Amonging, tgs:Concept.DunbarsNumber, tgs:Concept.DarkTriad ;
    thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary "Different in character from RR-1 and RR-24's convergence-through-complementary-expertise — here the three guests are colleagues at the same institute, with Nora as the clear intellectual center (founder, coiner of the terms being discussed) and Jonathan and Lucas mostly contributing illustrative, experiential texture (a son's skating community, tea-making, a shared meal ritual, a teenage trip to see the Dalai Lama) rather than distinct or competing positions. What makes this episode distinct is that the conversation doesn't just describe the practice it's about — unscripted, non-competitive, deeply attentive listening — it visibly performs it; Nate explicitly notices this happening in real time more than once, remarking that none of the guests give him an immediate reflexive answer and that the best parts of the conversation were when he 'forgot I was a podcast host.' The closest thing to genuine friction is Nate's own pointed challenge — if a community selects for openness, curiosity, integrity, and generosity, wouldn't it simply get exploited or outcompeted by people with Dark Triad traits? — which the three guests work through collaboratively rather than by disagreeing with each other, building out Jonathan's 'lattice work' image together (bad-faith actors can't get purchase because the group's structure keeps moving). Nate also brings real personal vulnerability into the conversation himself, using his own unresolved dishes-with-a-partner example, which Nora then uses live to illustrate 'systems holdback' (a form of schismogenesis) rather than treating it as a hypothetical. The episode closes with two real, direct corroborations of facts already in this graph: Nora naming her new book, Belly, and confirming that she's appeared on the show more than anyone except Daniel Schmachtenberger."@en ;
    dct:references tgs:PanelDiscussion.RR_19_DarkTriad, tgs:PanelDiscussion.RR_10_TheEcologyOfCommunication, tgs:Monologue.Frankly_88_SnowSingularityRocksInTheRiver, tgs:Monologue.Frankly_23_MordorEconomy, tgs:Monologue.Frankly_108_TheInfluenceOfPsychopaths, tgs:Interview.TGS_191_TaylorGuthrie, tgs:Work.RealityBlind, tgs:Work.Belly, tgs:Work.Combining ;
    skos:scopeNote "RESOLVED 2026-08-13, via MJSullivan's own real extracted link list from this episode's real show notes: RadicalHospitality and Amonging both now built as real Concepts (were previously judged borderline in this same scopeNote), Dark Triad (RR-19) and the schismogenesis Reality Roundtable (RR-10) both now built as real Episode stubs (were previously flagged deferred). Daniel Schmachtenberger's own further TGS appearances and Nora Bateson's own extensive further TGS history remain real but still deferred beyond what's captured here."@en ;
    .

tgs:PanelDiscussion.RR_27_AnEcologicalPsychology a thinkr:Episode, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:createdDate "2026-08-13"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:updatedDate "2026-08-13"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.PanelDiscussion ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeSegment tgs:EpisodeSegment.RealityRoundtable ;
    skos:prefLabel "An Ecological Psychology: Internal Family Systems for a Turbulent World"@en ;
    skos:definition "Nate is joined by Richard Schwartz, creator of Internal Family Systems (IFS) — the therapeutic model describing the mind as naturally composed of 'parts' (exiles, managers, firefighters) organized around an undamageable core 'Self' — and climate psychologist Steffi Bednarek, who extends the same model outward to organizations, governments, and whole cultures. The conversation moves from IFS fundamentals through Nate's own explicit application of the model to his 'economic Superorganism' framing, to Bednarek's real work running Systems Wayfinding sessions with governments and wealth holders, and closes on the spiritual/non-dual dimension of the 'Self' both guests have independently arrived at."@en ;
    dct:created "2026-08-05"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2026-08-12"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    thinkr:hasGuest tgs:Persona.RichardSchwartz, tgs:Persona.SteffiBednarek ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/episode/reality-roundtable-27> ;
    thinkr:hasTranscript <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/08/RR-27-Transcript-An-Ecological-Psychology.pdf> ;
    thinkr:discusses tgs:Concept.Superorganism, tgs:Concept.DarkTriad, tgs:Concept.GrowthImperative ;
    dct:references tgs:Monologue.Frankly_97_TheSuperorganismIn7Minutes, tgs:PanelDiscussion.RR_19_DarkTriad, tgs:Persona.VanessaAndreotti ;
    skos:scopeNote "Unusually rich in real, direct cross-references to already-built content in this graph: the episode's own real show notes cite Frankly_97 ('The Superorganism in 7 Minutes') by name for the Economic Superorganism concept, RR-19 by name for the Dark Triad discussion, and Vanessa Andreotti's own real prior TGS episode (#125, via the term 'Hospicing Modernity') — all three already real, built individuals in this graph before this episode was processed. Also real and directly confirmed: Francis Weller's own prior TGS episode (#167) and Xiye Bastida's (#208), both cited directly by Nate from personal experience but not built as Episode individuals in this pass. Frankly #123 ('The Consumption Pyramid') is directly confirmed as the completed version of the 'Frankly on public choice' Nate says he is 'working on' mid-conversation — real evidence this episode predates that Frankly's publication."@en ;
    thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary "The longest and most extended conversation among all built RRs — Nate's own words near the close: 'coming up on two hours, this is one of the longer conversations I've had this year.' Schwartz and Bednarek are visibly not just co-guests but active collaborators already deep in an ongoing dialogue of their own ('this is a conversation that I am currently having with Dick and I really love it,' Bednarek says at one point) — the complementary split is genuinely structural rather than just topical: Schwartz supplies the clinical, individual-level IFS framework directly, while Bednarek supplies its real extension into organizations, governments, and whole cultures via her own Systems Wayfinding work. The one moment of real, pointed challenge in the episode comes from Nate himself, not between the guests — he directly names the skepticism his own audience is likely to feel toward psychological frameworks given the podcast's usual material/energy/systems focus, asking bluntly how IFS could actually affect a trajectory driven by biophysical limits rather than individual psychology. Both guests take the challenge seriously rather than deflecting: Bednarek is explicit that 'no psychological approach can stop the unfolding of biodiversity loss that is already underway,' reframing the value as being about how humans meet what's coming rather than preventing it; Schwartz answers instead with his own long-term, admittedly 'grandiose' goal of IFS replacing the dominant paradigm for relating to the mind entirely. The episode's most candid moment is Schwartz's own disclosed intellectual and spiritual evolution, told live rather than summarized: he began the work as a self-described 'scientific atheist' and has only this year become openly willing to describe the IFS 'Self' as a 'particle state' of something larger that different spiritual traditions have independently pointed to under different names. Nate's own participation is unusually reflexive throughout — he explicitly narrates applying the framework to himself in real time ('I feel like I've been in self-energy since this conversation started'), and Schwartz risks a genuinely vulnerable, self-deprecating aside about being triggered by his own wife, fully aware she would later watch the recording."@en ;
    .

